#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:10004
msgid "Step-by-Step Workflow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:10006
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"`Gal3D` mainly uses the {py:class}`Gal3DAnalyzer "
"<gal3d.analyzer.Gal3DAnalyzer>` class to build 3D galaxy models. "
"Initializing {py:class}`Gal3DAnalyzer <gal3d.analyzer.Gal3DAnalyzer>` "
"requires the following components:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:10008
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"{py:class}`Particles <gal3d.point.Particles>`, which initializes the "
"particle data and provides density estimates at arbitrary positions;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:10009
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"{py:class}`SphField <gal3d.field.spherical_field.field.SphField>`, which "
"constructs a smoothed density field and its associated interpolators from"
" the particle data;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:10010
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"{py:class}`Structure3D <gal3d.shape.Structure3D>`, which defines the "
"target shape, the error function, and related settings;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:10011
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"{py:class}`Optimizer <gal3d.optimization.optimizer.Optimizer>`, which "
"performs the parameter optimization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:20004
msgid "Particle Initialization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:20006
msgid ""
"The goal of particle initialization is to enable density estimation at "
"arbitrary positions from a particle distribution defined by positions and"
" masses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:20008
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"This section shows how to initialize the particle positions and masses "
"and construct a density-query object. The main class used here is "
"{py:class}`Particles <gal3d.point.Particles>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:20010
msgid ""
"To initialize the particle data, you need to provide the particle "
"positions (`pos`) and masses (`mass`). As in the previous examples, we "
"use particle data from a galaxy in the TNG50-1 simulation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:40002
msgid "Initializing the `Particles` Class"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:40004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Use the {py:class}`Particles <gal3d.point.Particles>` class to initialize"
" the particle positions and masses. The position array `pos` has shape "
"`(N, 3)`, and the mass array `mass` has shape `(N,)`, where `N` is the "
"number of particles."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:40008
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"For a more detailed introduction to {py:class}`Particles "
"<gal3d.point.Particles>`, including usage examples, see {ref}`Working "
"with Particle Classes <particles>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:70004
msgid "Constructing the Density Field"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:70006
msgid ""
"The purpose of this step is to build a density field once a density "
"estimator is available for arbitrary positions. Because direct density "
"queries can be computationally expensive, `Gal3D` constructs an "
"intermediate field representation to reduce the cost of repeated "
"evaluations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:70008
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Before constructing the density field, the particles must already be "
"initialized so that the density estimator is available. See "
"{ref}`Particle Initialization <particle-initialization>` for that step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:70010
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"This section focuses on building the density field with the "
"{py:class}`SphField <gal3d.field.spherical_field.field.SphField>` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:80002
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The `Sph` in {py:class}`SphField "
"<gal3d.field.spherical_field.field.SphField>` stands for *spherical*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:80004
msgid ""
"The basic idea of `SphField` is to cast rays outward from the center, "
"sample how the density varies along each ray, and construct a radial "
"density interpolator for each direction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:80006
msgid "Directions of the Density Probes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:80008
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Pass the initialized {py:class}`Particles <gal3d.point.Particles>` object"
" to {py:class}`SphField <gal3d.field.spherical_field.field.SphField>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:80010
msgid ""
"At the same time, specify the number of density probes and the sampling "
"method used to choose their directions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:100004
msgid ""
"In most cases, `ray_method='fibonacci'` provides more uniform angular "
"sampling than other methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:100006
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Uniform Sampling on the Sphere <spherical-sampling>` for a more"
" detailed discussion of the Fibonacci method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:100009
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"`particles` s the initialized particle object, that is, an instance "
"of{py:class}`Particles <gal3d.point.Particles>`，"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:100010
msgid ""
"`num_ray` is the number of density probes. Each probe traces how the "
"density changes outward from the center along one direction. The default "
"value is `1024`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:100011
msgid ""
"`ray_method` specifies how the probe directions are sampled. In most "
"cases, these directions should be distributed uniformly over the sphere. "
"The default is `fibonacci`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:110002
msgid "Radial Range of the Density Probes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:110004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"After choosing the direction of each density probe, we need to define its"
" radial range. This is done with {py:meth}`build_field_boundary "
"<gal3d.field.spherical_field.field.SphField.build_field_boundary>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:110006
msgid ""
"Both the minimum and maximum radii of each probe must be specified. Here,"
" we set the minimum radius to `0.2` and the maximum radius to the point "
"where the density falls to `2e4`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:130002
msgid ""
"After this step, you can inspect `field.inner_r` and `field.outer_r`. "
"These are one-dimensional arrays that give the inner and outer radius of "
"each density probe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:150002
msgid "The figure below shows the distributions of the inner and outer radii."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:170002
msgid "Sampling the Radial Density Profile"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:170004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Once the probe directions and radial ranges have been defined, each probe"
" must sample the density along its radial interval in order to recover "
"the radial density profile. This is done with "
"{py:meth}`build_profile_sample "
"<gal3d.field.spherical_field.field.SphField.build_profile_sample>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:170006
msgid ""
"Here we set `num_p=500` and `step_mode='log'`, which means that 500 "
"logarithmically spaced sample points are used along each direction to "
"trace the radial density distribution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:190002
msgid "Interpolating the Radial Density Profile"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:190004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"After sampling the radial density profile along each probe direction, the"
" next step is to construct interpolators from those samples. This is done"
" with {py:meth}`build_profile_interpolator "
"<gal3d.field.spherical_field.field.SphField.build_profile_interpolator>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:190006
msgid ""
"Here we set `interpolator_method='LU'`, which uses the Lower-Upper (LU) "
"method for monotonic interpolation. The option `is_decreasing=True` "
"specifies that the interpolator should be monotonic decreasing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:190011
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Monotonic Smooth Interpolation <monotonic-ray>` for details of "
"this implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:210002
msgid ""
"At this point, we have obtained a monotonic decreasing radial density "
"interpolator for each probe direction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:220002
msgid "Radial Distribution of Iso-Density Surfaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:220004
msgid ""
"The purpose of this step is to estimate the radial distribution of iso-"
"density surfaces and construct the corresponding interpolator, so that "
"parameter ranges can be constrained more effectively during model "
"fitting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:240002
msgid ""
"The figure below shows the estimated radial iso-density profile (black "
"points), the corresponding interpolation (red line), and the relative "
"error (green line)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:260004
msgid "Shape Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:260006
msgid "The purpose of this step is to define the target shape before fitting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:260008
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"This section focuses on shape setup using the {py:class}`Structure3D "
"<gal3d.shape.Structure3D>` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:270002
msgid "Initializing the Shape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:270004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"To initialize {py:class}`Structure3D <gal3d.shape.Structure3D>`, you need"
" to specify a shape function, a coordinate system, an error function, and"
" an error-evaluation method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:270006
msgid ""
"You can inspect all available options with "
"`Structure3D.available_options()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:300002
msgid ""
"Here we use `EulerShift`, which represents 3D coordinate transformations "
"using Euler angles and translations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:300004
msgid ""
"We also use `Ellipsoid_S`, an ellipsoidal shape function with shape "
"indices; `sums_dev_rscale`, a distance-squared-weighted error function; "
"and `isodensity_curve_dcall`, which evaluates distances to iso-density "
"surfaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:300006
msgid ""
"In the printed representation of `structure`, you can see `EulerShift` "
"and `Ellipsoid_S`, followed by their corresponding parameters and values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:310002
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"\\large\n"
"\\begin{array}{r c l}\n"
"& \\mathbf{Coordinate} & \\\\\n"
"\\text{Name} & : & \\large \\mathrm{EulerShift} \\\\\n"
"\\text{Func} & : & \\ \\mathbf{X}'=\\mathbf{X}\\,R_{zyx}^{\\mathsf "
"T}(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma)-\\mathbf{1}_n\\,\\mathbf{c} \\\\\n"
"\\text{Param (6)} & : & x_c=0.00,\\ y_c=0.00,\\ z_c=0.00,\\ "
"\\alpha=0.00,\\ \\beta=0.00,\\ \\gamma=0.00 \\\\\n"
"\\text{Derived\\ param} & : & \\ "
"\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}x_c&y_c&z_c\\end{bmatrix},\\ \\ "
"R_{zyx}(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma)=R_{z}(\\alpha)\\,R_{y}(\\beta)\\,R_{x}(\\gamma)"
" \\\\\n"
"& \\mathbf{Geometry} & \\\\\n"
"\\text{Name} & : & \\large \\mathrm{SuperEllipsoid} \\\\\n"
"\\text{Func} & : & ([(\\frac{x}{a})^2]^{S_a} + [(\\frac{y}{b})^2]^{S_b} +"
" [(\\frac{z}{c})^2]^{S_c}) \\\\\n"
"\\text{Param (6)} & : & a=3.00,\\ \\epsilon_{ab}=0.20,\\ "
"\\epsilon_{bc}=0.50,\\ S_a=1.00,\\ S_b=1.00,\\ S_c=1.00 \\\\\n"
"\\text{Derived\\ param} & : & b = a(1 - \\epsilon_{ab})=2.40,\\ c = b(1 -"
" \\epsilon_{bc})=1.20 \\\\\n"
"& \\mathbf{Error} & \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{Err func}   & : & \\mathrm{sums\\_dev\\_rscale} \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{Err method} & : & \\mathrm{isodensity\\_curve\\_dcall} \\\\\n"
"\\end{array}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:320004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"See {ref}`Superellipsoids with Different Shape Parameters <ellipsoid-"
"shape-show>` for visual examples of ellipsoids with different parameter "
"combinations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:320006
msgid ""
"The left panel below shows the `z=0` slice for the standard ellipsoid "
"case with shape indices `sa=1` and `sb=1`. Different colors correspond to"
" different values of `eps_ab`, illustrating how the ellipticity changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:320008
msgid ""
"The right panel shows how the shape indices affect the geometry. With "
"`eps_ab = 0.2`, different values of the shape indices produce different "
"shapes. When `sa=sb=1`, the result is a standard ellipsoid, and the slice"
" is a standard ellipse (black solid line). When `sa=sb=0.5`, the slice "
"becomes diamond-like (black dotted line). When `sa=sb=2`, the slice "
"becomes boxier and more rectangular (black dashed line)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:340002
msgid "Shape Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:340004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"`structure.parameters` is an instance of {py:class}`Parameters "
"<gal3d.optimization.parameter.Parameters>`, which stores all adjustable "
"parameters and their constraints."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:340008
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"`parameters` supports lower and upper bounds as well as additional "
"constraints. You can set lower and upper bounds with "
"{py:meth}`Parameters.set_lb "
"<gal3d.optimization.parameter.Parameters.set_lb>` and "
"{py:meth}`Parameters.set_ub "
"<gal3d.optimization.parameter.Parameters.set_ub>`. You can also add "
"equality constraints with {py:meth}`Parameters.add_equal_constraints "
"<gal3d.optimization.parameter.Parameters.add_equal_constraints>`. See "
"{ref}`Working with Parameter Classes <parameters>` for more detailed "
"examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:350002
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"\n"
"\\begin{array}{r c l}\n"
"\\mathbf{Parameters} & & \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{x} & : & 0.000 \\in [-0.200, 0.200] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{y} & : & 0.000 \\in [-0.200, 0.200] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{z} & : & 0.000 \\in [-0.200, 0.200] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{ang1} & : & 0.000 \\in [-3.142, 3.142] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{ang2} & : & 0.000 \\in [-1.571, 1.571] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{ang3} & : & 0.000 \\in [-3.142, 3.142] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{a} & : & 3.000 \\in [0.100, \\infty] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{eps\\_ab} & : & 0.200 \\in [0.001, 0.999] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{eps\\_bc} & : & 0.500 \\in [0.001, 0.999] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{sa} & : & 1.000 \\in [0.200, 2.000] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{sb} & : & 1.000 \\in [0.200, 2.000] \\\\\n"
"\\mathrm{sc} & : & 1.000 \\in [0.200, 2.000]\n"
"\\end{array}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:360002
msgid ""
"You can access the lower and upper bounds of all parameters through the "
"`parameters.lb` and `parameters.ub` attributes, both of which return "
"dictionaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:390002
msgid ""
"You can also inspect the lower and upper bounds of a specific parameter, "
"for example with `parameters['eps_bc'].lb` and `parameters['eps_bc'].ub`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:410004
msgid "Optimizer Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:410006
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The purpose of this step is to choose and configure an optimizer for "
"model fitting. Here we use the {py:class}`Optimizer "
"<gal3d.optimization.optimizer.Optimizer>` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:410008
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"You can list the available optimizer plugins with "
"{py:meth}`Optimizer.available_plugins "
"<gal3d.optimization.optimizer.Optimizer.available_plugins>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:430002
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"You can then retrieve a specific optimizer plugin with "
"{py:meth}`Optimizer.get_plugin "
"<gal3d.optimization.optimizer.Optimizer.get_plugin>`. This returns a "
"class derived from {py:class}`OptimizerBase "
"<gal3d.optimization.optimizer.OptimizerBase>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:450002
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"You can then inspect the optimization algorithms provided by this plugin "
"with {py:meth}`OptimizerBase.available_algorithm "
"<gal3d.optimization.optimizer.OptimizerBase.available_algorithm>` ."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:470002
msgid ""
"Next, choose one of the available algorithms, for example `\"trf\"`, and "
"use it to create an optimizer instance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:490003
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Additional optimizer settings can be configured with "
"{py:meth}`OptimizerBase.set_options "
"<gal3d.optimization.optimizer.OptimizerBase.set_options>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:500002
msgid "Running the Fit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:500004
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"After initializing {py:class}`Particles <gal3d.point.Particles>`, "
"{py:class}`SphField <gal3d.field.spherical_field.field.SphField>`, "
"{py:class}`Structure3D <gal3d.shape.Structure3D>`, and "
"{py:class}`Optimizer <gal3d.optimization.optimizer.Optimizer>`, pass them"
" to {py:class}`Gal3DAnalyzer <gal3d.analyzer.Gal3DAnalyzer>` to start the"
" fitting process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tutorials/step_by_step.ipynb:530002
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"You can then run the fit with {py:meth}`Gal3DAnalyzer.fit "
"<gal3d.analyzer.Gal3DAnalyzer.fit>`."
msgstr ""

